  27:  *
  28:  *  Debug message functionality.
  29:  *
  30:  *  To print a debug message, use debugmsg() or debugmsg_cpu(). The _cpu
  31:  *  variant is if the message comes from a particular CPU. In that case, the
  32:  *  name of the machine and cpu id may be printed.
  33:  *
  34:  *  In addition to the actual message to be printed, a subsystem, a component
  35:  *  name, and a verbosity level also need to be supplied. The component name
  36:  *  may be empty.
  37:  *
  38:  *  If it is relatively expensive to construct the message, then it would be
  39:  *  a bad idea to do that every time the debugmsg is called in case it is
  40:  *  rarely shown anyway (due to verbosity level being higher than the current
  41:  *  setting). Then, the following construct may be used to quickly check before
  42:  *  constructing the message that it will be shown:
  43:  *
  44:  *	if (ENOUGH_VERBOSITY(SUBSYS_XXX, VERBOSITY_YYY)) {
  45:  *		char msg[....];
  46:  *		// Construct msg here...
  47:  *
  48:  *		debugmsg(SUBSYS_XXX, "component", VERBOSITY_YYY, "%s", msg);
  49:  *	}
  50:  *
  51:  *
  52:  *  TODO:
  53:  *	New debugger commands:
